now we can use the unique path  characterization of trees to very  quickly figure out that every tree is  too colorable so we know that a tree is  a graph with unique paths between every  pair of vertices and as a consequence  the chromatic number of a tree with two  or more vertices is 2 the proof is just  to show you how to color it you're  killing clearly can't get by with one  color if you've got any two adjacent  vertices the two colorable way is that  you just choose an arbitrary vertex and  call it the root but you make the  arbitrary choice on what the route is  and there's a unique path from every  vertex from the root to every vertex  using this unique path characterization  and so we're just going to color  vertices by whether the path from the  root is of autor even length if it's an  even length color it red and if it's odd  length color it blue a colored green and  so we wind up alternating red and green  and the fact is that adjacent nodes are  going to be at a distance where 1 is an  odd distance and one as an even distance  which is why this method of coloring is  going to work a general property of two  coloring is that to figure out whether  or not a graph is through colorable and  how to do it is you just start picking  arbitrary vertex give it a color it red  and then color all the vertices adjacent  to it green and keep going in that way  coloring a vertex with a color different  from an adjacent vertex that's colored  until you get stuck if you don't get  stuck it's 2 colorable and if it's not 2  a colorable you're guaranteed to get  stuck so it's a very easy way to figure  out if a graph is 2 colorable another  characterization that of two color  ability in general is that a graph is  two colorable providing that all the  cycles that it has if any are of even  length of course a tree has no cycles so  that makes it 2 colorable for sure
 

